    {
      "id": "p0095-b003",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "Indeed, it turns out that one gray rock doesn’t want to be pushed. In English, the rock speaks with an unusual accent and uses words like “pardner” and “pumpkin” that give off a “cowboy” or “person from Texas” vibe.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.0805734,
          0.24,
          0.7088421,
          0.2957143
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.0805734,
          0.24,
          0.7088421,
          0.2957143
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "果然，有一块灰石头不愿意被推。在英文中，它说话带着特别的口音，会用 pardner（伙计）、pumpkin（南瓜，昵称） 这样的词，让人联想到牛仔，或是得克萨斯州的人。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": []
      },
      "layout": {
        "bbox": [
          0.0917925,
          0.2378571,
          0.7057824,
          0.3
        ],
        "font_size": 20,
        "line_height": 1.45,
        "font_weight": 450,
        "background": "transparent",
        "text_align": "justify"
      },
      "notes": "原图与截图保留；译文为待审校初稿。",
      "calibration": {
        "bbox": [
          0.0917925,
          0.2378571,
          0.7057824,
          0.3
        ],
        "font_size": 20,
        "leading": 29.0
      },
      "annotations": []
    },
    {
      "id": "p0095-b004",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "Toby noted that it wasn’t important for the Japanese script to retain this rock’s rural speech – the rock just needed to sound unique. Thus the rock now uses a distinctive speaking style associated with Edokko, a class of citizens who lived in Edo (now known as Tokyo) in the late 1700s and early 1800s. This speaking style evokes a special combination of assertive brashness and cheerful straightforwardness.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.0877128,
          0.3042857,
          0.7098621,
          0.4142857
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.0877128,
          0.3042857,
          0.7098621,
          0.4142857
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "Toby 说明，日文台词不必保留这块石头的乡土口音，只要让它说话与众不同就行。于是，日版采用了与 Edokko（江户子）相关的一种鲜明腔调。江户子是指 18 世纪末、19 世纪初生活在江户（今天的东京）的一类市民。这种说话方式把强势、莽撞与爽朗、直率揉合在一起，别有特色。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": [
          {
            "start": 49,
            "end": 55,
            "italic": true,
            "lang": "en"
          }
        ]
      },
